{:db/id 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186175741 :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-3x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1) = (6,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-6) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186176417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c97302ea-f8e7-5c70-95e0-5cc0b820ce24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186174740}] :question/id #uuid "a0a733e1-bbed-5414-830d-e413ae5ce0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-214033" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $x + 3y = 12$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id 17592186176432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9a0fd16-a7fb-50f3-b7af-08c3f7c388b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y =-5" :db/id 17592186174773} {:answer/id #uuid "e444bbf2-3489-5620-aec6-98d5c66e7208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - y =-4" :db/id 17592186174774} {:answer/id #uuid "1698b28e-f5ca-5014-9c8d-6e6be465ad74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + 3y = 0" :db/id 17592186174775} {:answer/id #uuid "22bc909f-d369-586b-a41d-d055ee4efd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y =-1" :db/id 17592186174776} {:answer/id #uuid "48da768f-c47d-520e-b1b9-201676db3161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y =-1" :db/id 17592186174777}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3db9b668-626e-53b0-85ef-b55b666f2a9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186174772}] :question/id #uuid "01fb643b-861f-5688-87eb-695c15d67442" :question/math-academy-id "q-23633" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$."} {:db/id 17592186238009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb0cd4-c595-52e2-b7b8-f5921f1664ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=-4" :db/id 17592186235858} {:answer/id #uuid "339a3f8d-ec60-53c0-b70c-2ad1dcb8beef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-10" :db/id 17592186236143} {:answer/id #uuid "bf918fbb-8ce0-57e4-ba33-761c02c1700f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=2" :db/id 17592186237036} {:answer/id #uuid "cd49d0d7-034b-5674-9bb1-76d06ca38e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=-6" :db/id 17592186237119} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id 17592186237390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id 17592186237390} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8d6f01-6ff4-56d4-9645-4daf3987bc75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186237683}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2cb582-f805-50f4-ae14-8678782b90fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-103449" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9cfaf6-9a1a-5e8a-ba94-ac0ace22d609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/id 17592186288209} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ae802-61bf-59f5-8b33-d414ac86732a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id 17592186288552} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9e515d-8988-5114-8e78-b9e0df04aced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/id 17592186288663} {:answer/id #uuid "6463122b-ca6b-5780-a23a-d3c73e619707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/id 17592186288741} {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id 17592186289064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id 17592186289064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec350e8b-707f-561e-b0a8-e7f213d56fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186290223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96f3c704-d5cd-55de-a550-62294d23b6d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d37221-efd8-5394-a3bf-5cd387534c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id 17592186288506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d37221-efd8-5394-a3bf-5cd387534c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id 17592186288506} :answer-field/id #uuid "66350c63-8aa4-5291-892a-860d45704838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0b26a33-738b-5140-8f04-e3933793a909" :question/math-academy-id "q-237230" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a038781-f6d7-5ad5-aee2-0340c847e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id 17592186288958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a038781-f6d7-5ad5-aee2-0340c847e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id 17592186288958} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a7209c-1afb-5996-b282-b1decb902920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289731}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "515eea7d-54c4-5f7a-8f42-e28d3350452e" :question/math-academy-id "q-237231" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186290502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7957bb33-46c9-58ef-8255-0b1ae5b1565b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id 17592186288868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7957bb33-46c9-58ef-8255-0b1ae5b1565b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id 17592186288868} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3516f9-effa-5511-95b1-9b2d4cea03d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186289749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd2e4750-a1c2-5012-bd49-32f6921fcef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-237232" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,1)$ and is perpendicular to $4x-3y=7?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $4x-3y=7$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =-4x+7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186292541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222e0bdd-c45c-5f9b-ae54-71cdb6646a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=2" :db/id 17592186290780} {:answer/id #uuid "93fc4b47-47c7-5ebf-9229-bb1de3d6fc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y=26" :db/id 17592186291272} {:answer/id #uuid "96366abc-617a-5664-be0a-124e181d78a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/id 17592186291284} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id 17592186291519} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c2f176-99d3-5c0e-91c7-0a77ab6cde83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y=19" :db/id 17592186291538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id 17592186291519} :answer-field/id #uuid "f929ad97-39dd-50e0-b2f2-34f577615f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186292514}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bf24f67-6184-5bd5-8b16-92247aa73ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-252524" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186341836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1988b7-4bc8-5bda-86c5-639e0552dedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=7" :db/id 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "51dac7ff-2d97-5661-b543-ad769758a707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=6" :db/id 17592186339880} {:answer/id #uuid "6d11b816-6f21-575c-a776-01785873cb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=5" :db/id 17592186340089} {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id 17592186340279} {:answer/id #uuid "af6755f9-b09b-562d-a589-5067ac2ec549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=7" :db/id 17592186340728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id 17592186340279} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ea68-a247-56ce-a08d-252af6a5df70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72571" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}
